rewdyer@outlook.com","name":"Andrew Dyer"},"message":"fix: emit a single header for hashed assets in nginx configs\n\nexpires 1y already sets Cache-Control: max-age=31536000, and the following\nadd_header Cache-Control \"public, immutable\" doesn''t replace it - nginx''s\nadd_header only adds headers, it never overrides ones set by other directives.\nConfirmed with a real response: /assets/ was sending two separate Cache-Control\nheader lines plus a redundant Expires header, which HTTP clients and proxies can\nhandle inconsistently.\n\nReplace both with a single add_header Cache-Control \"public, max-age=31536000,\nimmutable\" and drop expires entirely, since Cache-Control''s max-age already\ncovers what it provided. Same bug existed in both apps/web/nginx.conf and\npackages/shared/nginx.conf, since the Storybook config was copied from the app''s.\n\nVerified with fresh docker builds that each now sends exactly one Cache-Control\nheader, and ran nginx -t against both configs directly.","tree":{"sha":"56190ac4d3b758899eac4f0b1e01569e82c99642","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/andrewdyer/react-vite-turborepo-template/git/trees/56190ac4d3b758899eac4f0b1e01569e82c99642"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/andrewdyer/react-vite-turborepo-template/git/commits/f5e8de3f504359732a8ba0a03f43c19b9463617c","verification":{"payload":null,"reason":"unsigned","signature":null,"verified":false,"verified_at":null}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/8114523?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/andrewdyer/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/andrewdyer/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/andrewdyer/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/andrewdyer/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/andrewdyer","id":8114523,"login":"andrewdyer","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjgxMTQ1MjM=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/andrewdyer/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/andrewdyer/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/andrewdyer/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/andrewdyer/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/andrewdyer/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/andrewdyer","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/andrewdyer/react-vite-turborepo-template/commit/f5e8de3f504359732a8ba0a03f43c19b9463617c","node_id":"C_kwDOP7On7doAKGY1ZThkZTNmNTA0MzU5NzMyYThiYTBhMDNmNDNjMTliOTQ2MzYxN2M","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/andrewdyer/react-vite-turborepo-template/commit/c3637150ea1449d76e217f67f6eacda67dacdf36","sha":"c3637150ea1449d76e217f67f6eacda67dacdf36","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/andrewdyer/react-vite-turborepo-template/commits/c3637150ea1449d76e217f67f6eacda67dacdf36"}],"sha":"f5e8de3f504359732a8ba0a03f43c19b9463617c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/andrewdyer/react-vite-turborepo-template/commits/f5e8de3f504359732a8ba0a03f43c19b9463617c"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/8114523?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/andrewdyer/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/andrewdyer/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/andrewdyer/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/andrewdyer/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/andrewdyer","id":8114523,"login":"andrewdyer","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjgxMTQ1MjM=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/andrewdyer/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/andrewdyer/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/andrewdyer/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/andrewdyer/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/andrewdyer/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/andrewdyer","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/andrewdyer/react-vite-turborepo-template/commits/c3637150ea1449d76e217f67f6eacda67dacdf36/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-06T20:23:31Z","email":"andrewdyer@outlook.com","name":"Andrew Dyer"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-06T20:23:31Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"ci: add storybook publishing (#42)\n\n* chore: add dockerfile and nginx config for publishing storybook\n\nAdd Dockerfile.storybook, a root-level Dockerfile that builds and serves any\npackage''s Storybook via the same prune -> install -> build -> nginx pattern the\napp Dockerfile uses. It takes two build args instead of one: PACKAGE_NAME (the\nnpm name, e.g. \"@repo/shared\") for turbo prune, which requires the package.json\nname field rather than a path, and PACKAGE_PATH (the workspace directory, e.g.\n\"packages/shared\") for locating the build output and nginx config - a split the\napp Dockerfile doesn''t need since an app''s directory name always matches its\npackage name.\n\nAdd packages/shared/nginx.conf alongside it. It isn''t a copy of the app SPA\nconfig: Storybook navigates between stories with a `?path=` query string on a\nsingle page rather than distinct routes, so there''s no SPA fallback to\nindex.html for arbitrary paths. It still needs `try_files $uri $uri/ =404` so\ndirectory requests like `/` resolve through the index directive, and keeps the\nsame immutable caching for hashed /assets/ files and no-cache on the two\nnon-hashed entry points, index.html and iframe.html.\n\nNeither file is wired into a workflow yet - nothing builds or pushes these\nimages until the publish workflow is updated to call them.\n\n* feat: publish storybook images alongside apps\n\nWire Dockerfile.storybook into publish.yml: after a packages/ release, check\nwhether the package defines a build:storybook script - independent of its\nprivate field, since a package can skip npm publishing but still want a\nbrowsable Storybook - and if so, build and push\nghcr.io/{repo}-{package}-storybook to GHCR alongside whatever else that\nrelease already does.\n\nReuse the existing deploy/rollback machinery apps already have rather than\nbuilding a parallel path for it. Baking the \"-storybook\" suffix directly into\nthe image name means deploy.yml needs no changes at all: it already resolves\nghcr.io/{repo}-{app} generically from whatever app string it''s given, so a\ndispatch payload of app: \"shared-storybook\" produces the right image name with\nno branching logic anywhere. rollback.yml just gets \"shared-storybook\" added\nto the app dropdown''s options alongside \"web\", using the same resolution.\n\n* fix: pin turbo versions in dockerfiles\n\nPinned turbo to version 2.10.4 to match the version already resolved in package.json.\n\n* refactor: use pnpm exec turbo\n\npnpm turbo relies on pnpm''s fallback of resolving an unrecognized top-level command\nagainst node_modules/.bin, which is what makes it work here - turbo is a workspace\ndevDependency, not just the global install from earlier in the image. That fallback\nisn''t obvious even to an attentive reader; it read as broken in PR review despite\nworking correctly. pnpm exec turbo is the explicit form of the exact same resolution,\nalready used elsewhere in this repo (test.yml''s `pnpm --filter web-e2e exec\nplaywright install`), so this changes nothing about behavior, only how obviously\ncorrect it reads. Verified with a clean --no-cache build of both Dockerfiles.\n\n* fix: emit a single header for hashed assets in nginx configs\n\nexpires 1y already sets Cache-Control: max-age=31536000, and the following\nadd_header Cache-Control \"public, immutable\" doesn''t replace it - nginx''s\nadd_header only adds headers, it never overrides ones set by other directives.\nConfirmed with a real response: /assets/ was sending two separate Cache-Control\nheader lines plus a redundant Expires header, which HTTP clients and proxies can\nhandle inconsistently.\n\nReplace both with a single add_header Cache-Control \"public, max-age=31536000,\nimmutable\" and drop expires entirely, since Cache-Control''s max-age already\ncovers what it provided. Same bug existed in both apps/web/nginx.conf and\npackages/shared/nginx.conf, since the Storybook config was copied from the app''s.\n\nVerified with fresh docker builds that each now sends exactly one Cache-Control\nheader, and ran nginx -t against both configs directly.","tree":{"sha":"ba647d4a9e4effcbe47df444c1e616015620141e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/andrewdyer/react-vite-turborepo-template/git/trees/ba647d4a9e4effcbe47df444c1e616015620141e"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/andrewdyer/react-vite-turborepo-template/git/commits/c3637150ea1449d76e217f67f6eacda67dacdf36","verification":{"payload":"tree ba647d4a9e4effcbe47df444c1e616015620141e\nparent 4c671f22d3143de43b6c4a1a341a14c652093f2c\nauthor Andrew Dyer <andrewdyer@outlook.com> 1786047811 +0100\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786047811 +0100\n\nci: add storybook publishing (#42)\n\n* chore: add dockerfile and nginx config for publishing storybook\n\nAdd Dockerfile.storybook, a root-level Dockerfile that builds and serves any\npackage''s Storybook via the same prune -> install -> build -> nginx pattern the\napp Dockerfile uses. It takes two build args instead of one: PACKAGE_NAME (the\nnpm name, e.g. \"@repo/shared\") for turbo prune, which requires the package.json\nname field rather than a path, and PACKAGE_PATH (the workspace directory, e.g.\n\"packages/shared\") for locating the build output and nginx config - a split the\napp Dockerfile doesn''t need since an app''s directory name always matches its\npackage name.\n\nAdd packages/shared/nginx.conf alongside it. It isn''t a copy of the app SPA\nconfig: Storybook navigates between stories with a `?path=` query string on a\nsingle page rather than distinct routes, so there''s no SPA fallback to\nindex.html for arbitrary paths. It still needs `try_files $uri $uri/ =404` so\ndirectory requests like `/` resolve through the index directive, and keeps the\nsame immutable caching for hashed /assets/ files and no-cache on the two\nnon-hashed entry points, index.html and iframe.html.\n\nNeither file is wired into a workflow yet - nothing builds or pushes these\nimages until the publish workflow is updated to call them.\n\n* feat: publish storybook images alongside apps\n\nWire Dockerfile.storybook into publish.yml: after a packages/ release, check\nwhether the package defines a build:storybook script - independent of its\nprivate field, since a package can skip npm publishing but still want a\nbrowsable Storybook - and if so, build and push\nghcr.io/{repo}-{package}-storybook to GHCR alongside whatever else that\nrelease already does.\n\nReuse the existing deploy/rollback machinery apps already have rather than\nbuilding a parallel path for it. Baking the \"-storybook\" suffix directly into\nthe image name means deploy.yml needs no changes at all: it already resolves\nghcr.io/{repo}-{app} generically from whatever app string it''s given, so a\ndispatch payload of app: \"shared-storybook\" produces the right image name with\nno branching logic anywhere. rollback.yml just gets \"shared-storybook\" added\nto the app dropdown''s options alongside \"web\", using the same resolution.\n\n* fix: pin turbo versions in dockerfiles\n\nPinned turbo to version 2.10.4 to match the version already resolved in package.json.\n\n* refactor: use pnpm exec turbo\n\npnpm turbo relies on pnpm''s fallback of resolving an unrecognized top-level command\nagainst node_modules/.bin, which is what makes it work here - turbo is a workspace\ndevDependency, not just the global install from earlier in the image. That fallback\nisn''t obvious even to an attentive reader; it read as broken in PR review despite\nworking correctly. pnpm exec turbo is the explicit form of the exact same resolution,\nalready used elsewhere in this repo (test.yml''s `pnpm --filter web-e2e exec\nplaywright install`), so this changes nothing about behavior, only how obviously\ncorrect it reads. Verified with a clean --no-cache build of both Dockerfiles.\n\n* fix: emit a single header for hashed assets in nginx configs\n\nexpires 1y already sets Cache-Control: max-age=31536000, and the following\nadd_header Cache-Control \"public, immutable\" doesn''t replace it - nginx''s\nadd_header only adds headers, it never overrides ones set by other directives.\nConfirmed with a real response: /assets/ was sending two separate Cache-Control\nheader lines plus a redundant Expires header, which HTTP clients and proxies can\nhandle inconsistently.\n\nReplace both with a single add_header Cache-Control \"public, max-age=31536000,\nimmutable\" and drop expires entirely, since Cache-Control''s max-age already\ncovers what it provided. Same bug existed in both apps/web/nginx.conf and\npackages/shared/nginx.conf, since the Storybook config was copied from the app''s.\n\nVerified with fresh docker builds that each now sends exactly one Cache-Control\nheader, and ran nginx -t against both configs directly.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqdO1DCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAi7kQAA4smL1QWNLu+ynDt01GvfLH\nXDsc3acsx7Q3TQqaJaT/ZOeyHEJKNZc32T2NC5xG66uKQOBC9gv+IpH2qc9iv19T\n2hJbzEUzm5paKLaAol0LAq+dPatrEjp+biWwRyG3e4M0i/QNXe8OWKY5ya3o+LmD\nqv2AsWItjdxOX0K2TOfzvbRlTQHa3bbwUuBVW7fmoHNEMQWJygPo8CDTUVFh+yJz\nbbtxQ8s43hNDPxEbqzwXKtOoqxrlOH/wUmBt59/sR5rmtu4Yhlw0ydUs8bOq2r+m\nd5caHuikgHk1dsGGpmXeCDXOoeZXyo6mlUZ6Dxv0uYKmrdVADZ1KdW1hjxPacr1u\nkbDPFQOP9lJnAO1tOKoGzrX5uhgAFEEVqY9ULyG+NWiRNA8IhleeLXLbqfn/yJOT\nRtSnh7P2GNLdzGH9gsvzpHc148qk8hCFXprdi88/ehXicPvXfVf4ChuNN3rLQSM9\nAknNdQRMlEQQD36bSh0mvXaMU+N7b3/JlJU/Achp5TPsHZGf1IfvWo+jgwhuTm4+\nBHy8Msq9Nla81ecAbQlcZboG5UOqRrjcSWDdhv/76UjneQL99gycvzr5Dx9EmkA4\npXjIPCkCbTc6Hl849ZnwA4FBVcsDyanXpmCmiVeFUxp5JjIA4OpVB3Pe7oaLIAeU\nfypBipyYCicSt5